%[text] %[text:anchor:T_CFF3DFB5] # Air Track Collisions Lab: Energy Conversion
%[text] [⇦ Return to the main menu](<matlab: open(fullfile(currentProject().RootFolder,'MainMenu.m'))>)
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_78CFA2E7] ## Part 1: Energy Conversion, Unequal Mass
%[text] 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_50C67419] ### Step 1. Conduct Experiment
%[text] 1. Open the Simscape Multibody model: [airTrackLab](<matlab: open(fullfile(currentProject().RootFolder,'Models/airTrackLab.slx'))>).
%[text] 2. Double-click the air track image to open the dialog. Configure the lab to add a hanging mass by pressing the button **3. Energy**.
%[text] 3. Verify that no mass has been added to cart 1, 200 g has been added to cart 2, and that the hanging mass has 100 g.
%[text] 4. Verify that cart 2 will start at $x = 0${"altText":"x = 0"}, gate 1 is placed at $x = 0.3${"altText":"x = 0.3"}, and gate 2 at $x = 0.6${"altText":"x = 0.6"}.
%[text] 5. Start the simulation.
%[text] 6. Stop the simulation after the cart has passed through both gate 1 and gate 2. \
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Block parameters dialog for the air track model. Select 3. Energy to add a hanging mass.](text:image:7fb7)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Block parameters dialog for the air track model. Select* ***3. Energy*** *to add a hanging mass.*
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_323AC2AF] ### Step 2: Record Measurements
%[text] 1. Record the amount of time cart 2 is detected by the sensor in gate 1.
%[text] 2. Record the amount of time cart 2 is detected by the sensor in gate 2.
%[text] 3. Compute the velocity at each position and record them below. \
%[text] **Note**: replace **NaN** with your result.
% Stop clock times
t1_p4 = 1.2052;
t2_p4 = 0.2020;
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_04D211EF] ### Step 3: Calculate Velocities at Each Gate
%[text] If you are not sure how to calculate the velocity from the stop clock times times, consult the [Pre-Lab](<matlab: open(fullfile(currentProject().RootFolder,'InstructorResources/Solutions/PrelabSoln.m'))>) for an example. Recall that the cart is 0.15 m long.
% Calculate velocities before collision (in m/s)
v1_p4 = 0.15/t1_p4;
v2_p4 = 0.15/t2_p4;
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_1B1F182A] ### Step 4: Analyze Energy
%[text] For the initial state, the time the cart passes through gate 1, and the time the cart passes through gate 2, compute:
%[text] 1. Potential energy
%[text] 2. Kinetic energy
%[text] 3. Total energy \
%[text] **Note**: 
%[text] - Each cart has a mass of 180 g = 0.18 kg (+ any added mass).
%[text] - The hook and cable are massless. \
mc = (180+200)*10^-3; % Cart mass
mh = 50*10^-3; % Hanging mass
v0 = 0;
v1 = 0.15/0.1912;
v2 = 0.15/0.1323;

% initial state
ep0_da4 = mh*9.81*0.9       % potential energy (J)
ek0_da4 = 0                 % kinetic energy (J)
e0_da4 = ep0_da4 + ek0_da4  % total energy (J)

% state 1: passing through gate 1
ep1_da4 = mh*9.81*0.6       % potential energy (J)
ek1_da4 = 0.5*(mc+mh)*v1^2  % kinetic energy (J)
e1_da4 = ep1_da4 + ek1_da4  % total energy (J)

% state 2: passing through gate 2
ep2_da4 = mh*9.81*0.3       % potential energy (J)
ek2_da4 = 0.5*(mc+mh)*v2^2  % kinetic energy (J)
e2_da4 = ep2_da4 + ek2_da4  % total energy (J)
%[text] **Question.** Should the total energy be conserved in this system? Do the observations match what you expect?
q_da4 = "Yes. Yes, the observations show that the energy is mostly conserved. The losses are consistent with frictional losses.";
%[text] [⇦ Return to the main menu](<matlab: open(fullfile(currentProject().RootFolder,'MainMenu.m'))>)
